#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162849} :activity/id #uuid "22c8da45-56d1-5645-85df-aa94cccb29d8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049849 :topic/id #uuid "1887f465-a1b8-5370-82f6-80e02b9d153a" :topic/math-academy-id 3120} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162849 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163224 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $\\mathbb{R}.$ The **spectral theorem** states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- The eigenvectors corresponding to *different* eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable." :tutorial/id #uuid "5ecbbdd0-25a0-52ee-8f0c-987dcd8ba584" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4882 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "84b2f14e-f6c7-5b6c-b6d7-19d4d7d71f71" :step/math-academy-id 30533 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161805 :question/id #uuid "6ae71f41-133f-50b6-a286-20ae7e2c3a9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8291"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c67d532-acd9-5231-a27f-d39084863e01" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048493}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478420}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Spectral Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "95f72e96-f84b-5b21-a070-253638be57a8" :step/math-academy-id 30970 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163243 :tutorial/content "We can write an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix $A$ in terms of a sum of products of its eigenvalues and eigenvectors.\n\nSpecifically, if $\\mathbf{u}_i$ denotes an orthonormal eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_i$ for $i=1,\\ldots, n$, then\n\n$$\nA=\\sum_{i=1}^n \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T.\n$$\n\nExpressing a symmetric matrix $A$ in this way is called the **spectral decomposition** of $A.$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the spectral decomposition of the $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/40/408e7471e4ef788758d423af97f5683186f79192fd8933c350d45bb87f089875.svg)\n\nThe eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1 = 6$ and $\\lambda_2 = -4,$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are\n\n![](images/0c/0c22f7dd97e83c358cb3cef1fcc2db902d908140109334a8cf1c22b35b29c6d2.svg)\n\nThe spectral decomposition of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA =\\sum_{i=1}^2 \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1 \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2 \\mathbf u_2^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spectral decomposition of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{6}_{⏟}}_{{\\lambda }_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{10} & \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{9}{10}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}}+{{(-4)}_{⏟}}_{{\\lambda }_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{10} & -\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this decomposition, the matrix $\\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T$ is the orthogonal projection matrix that maps any $\\mathbf x \\in \\mathbb R^2$ onto the subspace spanned by $\\mathbf u_i.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a90957e3-e4b9-548d-930b-e060c6b63859" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4973 :tutorial/title "The Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "8df507ec-4d53-59f1-b941-07907cd14b6f" :step/math-academy-id 30971 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162014 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161806 :question/id #uuid "d185ef42-c177-5217-99b7-b42d800f3385" :question/math-academy-id "e-8292"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049489}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495170}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"} :step/id #uuid "2a1d5d86-333c-5361-87ec-fd7766797988" :step/math-academy-id 30972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162015 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161807 :question/id #uuid "65b23c21-528f-5df7-a00c-8e2dce041276" :question/math-academy-id "e-8293"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407553}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "86248bdd-6a75-54cd-8196-8b333a64cac5" :step/math-academy-id 30973 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162904}}] :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162216}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407555} :activity/id #uuid "cb802846-7e96-5e29-a7fd-43a85cc938d8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049849 :topic/id #uuid "1887f465-a1b8-5370-82f6-80e02b9d153a" :topic/math-academy-id 3120} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "237e9827-741e-554d-b4bd-a580c12832ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407524} {:answer/id #uuid "637ff16c-2a36-5623-9258-1f3130b80075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407529} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8f4850-6613-5ef7-a355-f12825aba902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407532} {:answer/id #uuid "bea7dec3-53c9-52fb-9b61-30210ef0b89a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407538} {:answer/id #uuid "d23ed5d0-4d06-561d-902d-1213f1013134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407524} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b7eee37-4841-5d7e-83f1-ad47a007de82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "69f0b9bd-9336-5c1c-91e3-bf72b33b1333" :question/math-academy-id "q-140847" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+8\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+8\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "07386288-c5f3-55df-b73a-99cec25cb7d6"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1556dc1b-0c8b-54e0-a340-18371f592ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407520} {:answer/id #uuid "286d06a8-1050-59ed-a665-558d840c2aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407525} {:answer/id #uuid "d6effcc8-e643-5b20-9090-b3855faacab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407541} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f4f75f-447a-5e0c-a1d6-feae985a30a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407543} {:answer/id #uuid "f93a6cf9-78b6-546d-ba88-361fc48a056a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407544}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407544} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebade115-5b0c-52d7-94cc-8ec9e5babad9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11bdef24-ff68-513b-bfa6-5f2fb8c78b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-94768" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2d35b336-2654-53f7-8116-4689735d233e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407557}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16877fa0-d3a3-5da8-ba77-c7e71244a7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407521} {:answer/id #uuid "6d264661-257d-5c8a-8684-122b5c0a6807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407531} {:answer/id #uuid "9555f6b0-dd96-561e-b956-a28b10613e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407535} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0b779d-af50-58f8-97aa-dd75027108dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407536} {:answer/id #uuid "d6231cbd-de79-555a-b056-b8008595ed25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407531} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1092e23-b9a3-5556-b0d1-0745f95be4b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7e3f233e-913d-50cf-8912-1c6832afca12" :question/math-academy-id "q-95725" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-65 & 78 \\\\ 78 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5fc71f89-cc9a-549d-9f1f-b3a2081e4a63" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407554}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d327dec-d42a-581c-8707-926d0b6893a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407522} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3ec327-8643-5dfe-8735-8c0774db03ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407526} {:answer/id #uuid "799755e8-7ecf-5b56-8460-da2805b858a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407533} {:answer/id #uuid "95054c16-a500-55cc-9c6d-5dc257b93966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407534} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b2cd88-0c1c-5067-96b8-f34a70eda38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407542} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb85388f-97d4-5b7a-9384-bb45d3a9d42b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9d231abd-db3d-5d11-9253-820e7eb51742" :question/math-academy-id "q-95712" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 18\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 18\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 16 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "61132d09-e84e-51b9-b83a-aedca27c1563" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407558}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f090535-e10a-5c59-9a51-2d2aae1cbc9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407523} {:answer/id #uuid "3f43beb7-1a8e-5d63-bf78-07894e8c2c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407527} {:answer/id #uuid "5613d7c8-bedb-54d2-be91-7e80b596eb5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407528} {:answer/id #uuid "694df5b3-0ed3-5486-8f0d-63bf6d2b9e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407530} {:answer/id #uuid "bc550768-52c7-5626-83cb-470760bd86c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407537}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407523} :answer-field/id #uuid "a49b921e-a620-50dc-993e-f6b1a37ca3f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b502656e-ef35-5f6c-921c-ae3a8ea1f2a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-95721" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-6,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-25 & 10 \\\\ 10 & -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "639ff62f-ac48-56c2-bf2b-580bf31ccf01" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407556}}] :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407519}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495258} :activity/id #uuid "f7834130-0260-5c43-bf45-c2988a6f854e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049849 :topic/id #uuid "1887f465-a1b8-5370-82f6-80e02b9d153a" :topic/math-academy-id 3120} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495205 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a866929-0beb-5a32-8959-34966c6e6ad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494370} {:answer/id #uuid "28cc6daa-99e2-5439-a52d-8c74f6f8d55b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494393} {:answer/id #uuid "7d73673d-5635-5b93-8b05-960db3e3f68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494590} {:answer/id #uuid "891a910a-dee7-5f7a-b066-db75538859fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494623} {:answer/id #uuid "fec25ec0-ae75-52d4-992b-cbf821a92df5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494915}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494915} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f1f6569-2671-58ca-aa36-0fefdf95c894" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f8b0f486-8442-538c-933a-d85ad556ff51" :question/math-academy-id "q-95716" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 17\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 17\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 16 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=5.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "67e20c48-efe2-5fd4-8d06-cb1125f84345" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495243}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495243 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddb77fdb-9e1a-59d2-a520-e52d80297bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378067} {:answer/id #uuid "20a24a23-34cd-59e2-919c-ffa350d8078c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378068} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f8cd17-d0e2-5147-8618-30de79a463fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378069} {:answer/id #uuid "5649503f-0459-5e9f-9fcf-d4672d0737b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378070} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6a63c9-7482-5e58-94cf-dffcb3862ae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378071}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378069} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ed15ba3-7536-51e1-aa4c-32ec9da0175e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e3527660-7336-5854-9fe8-3ed70b1dd1cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-95723" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-40 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 40\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b38fd098-3465-5c13-8a12-93f1b35b5090" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495260}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495253 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162662 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07faab27-15f7-5312-8b38-59ad02f72521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -25\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494338} {:answer/id #uuid "2f44ec68-5673-5d17-a202-807989a0d5ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -25\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494410} {:answer/id #uuid "6c89eafa-421f-5962-9af4-946ba03a1964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494540} {:answer/id #uuid "8b205150-ffac-59f3-b8f8-30947cc22faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494635} {:answer/id #uuid "9d276ed0-9aab-5f35-92de-cd40b3be996e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494678} :answer-field/id #uuid "27a49c53-38b7-5dbb-b5f4-f47add37c5d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "28c27a06-cd3b-5e3d-a19e-4b46b9bccd18" :question/math-academy-id "q-95714" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-10$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-10.$ Computing $A-(-10)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+10I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{bmatrix}+10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 25\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 25 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-5{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{26}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cd9e99be-c8ca-50d9-aacd-09344ae9dae2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495205}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495258 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162647 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9efaaf0-779f-5a72-aa61-4761f6295dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366582} {:answer/id #uuid "5d20e65d-8c96-5a7e-8fea-9024d97dc82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366583} {:answer/id #uuid "d2283d99-cd32-55e5-9365-da3163600171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366584} {:answer/id #uuid "1172809a-fec9-5ba9-a260-4a0f08543d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366585} {:answer/id #uuid "d89d3c9b-2928-5750-a596-9fb110e7f349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366586} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4531768-c85a-5927-b9ea-dd6e8018f412" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "48eccd0e-cb0a-5561-99a4-875f56fc9de1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94769" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-8)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-8)\\cdot \\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}(9\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-8)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}(9\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}16 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-8)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}136 & -68 \\\\ -68 & -119\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d7e437ea-03ba-522c-8c2f-e2da5dacae70" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495253}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495260 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d17ddb9-9738-5247-980d-6346f34ca3d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395068} {:answer/id #uuid "9112c274-741d-5926-a7e8-28550395e3fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395069} {:answer/id #uuid "a6833e0f-d872-5f84-8fad-2cd587942a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395070} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a1eeb9-2422-526b-996e-3116b894cad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395071} {:answer/id #uuid "bb3216f5-0141-54bf-b967-4b7d4a78081b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395072}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395068} :answer-field/id #uuid "da07241e-4465-5487-a804-a1f93208770d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4f741f54-e9ba-55b4-9c81-df73745d51c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-95724" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dc9cbb5e-30fb-5f9a-8c8a-71b1f6f7926c"}] :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494318}]}
